WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.835
Begin by visualizing two distinct execution
environments operating simultaneously on the

2
00:00:05.835 --> 00:00:06.887
same hardware.

3
00:00:06.887 --> 00:00:13.201
User mode handles everyday application logic,
while kernel mode manages hardware resources

4
00:00:13.201 --> 00:00:15.306
and enforces security boundaries.

5
00:00:15.306 --> 00:00:21.037
When a user program needs to access protected
services, it deliberately triggers a system

6
00:00:21.037 --> 00:00:23.903
call that transfers control into the kernel.

7
00:00:23.903 --> 00:00:28.711
This deliberate handoff prevents unauthorized
hardware manipulation.

8
00:00:28.711 --> 00:00:34.217
Imagine a web browser requesting a network
socket; the browser code cannot directly talk

9
00:00:34.217 --> 00:00:35.790
to the network card.

10
00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:43.071
Instead, it issues a system call, causing the
CPU to switch to kernel mode, where the OS

11
00:00:43.071 --> 00:00:46.926
validates the request and executes the
appropriate hardware driver.

12
00:00:46.926 --> 00:00:53.285
This separation ensures that a buggy
application cannot crash the entire system or

13
00:00:53.285 --> 00:00:55.242
access another program's data.

14
00:00:55.242 --> 00:01:00.752
The kernel acts as a strict gatekeeper, only
allowing operations that follow defined,

15
00:01:00.866 --> 00:01:06.645
Next, observe how the kernel manages multiple
applications running on a single processor.

16
00:01:06.645 --> 00:01:12.702
When one program finishes its current task or
waits for input, the scheduler must pause it

17
00:01:12.702 --> 00:01:13.838
and start another.

18
00:01:13.838 --> 00:01:16.759
This transition is called a context switch.

19
00:01:16.759 --> 00:01:22.965
During this moment, the kernel saves the
complete register state of the paused process,

20
00:01:22.965 --> 00:01:25.624
including instruction pointers and stack
addresses.

21
00:01:25.624 --> 00:01:29.247
It then restores the saved registers of the
next process.

22
00:01:29.247 --> 00:01:35.297
Think of it like a theater stage manager who
saves every actor's costume and notes before

23
00:01:35.297 --> 00:01:36.810
calling the next performer.

24
00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:43.014
If the kernel failed to save these registers,
the next program would resume with corrupted

25
00:01:43.014 --> 00:01:44.668
data or crash immediately.

26
00:01:44.668 --> 00:01:51.310
The scheduler continuously evaluates which
process is ready to run, ensuring fair CPU

27
00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:53.354
distribution without manual intervention.

28
00:01:53.354 --> 00:01:59.476
This automatic handoff keeps the system
responsive even when dozens of applications

29
00:01:59.476 --> 00:01:59.986
compete.

30
00:02:00.146 --> 00:02:05.784
Consider how the CPU actually recognizes when
to pause user code and enter the kernel.

31
00:02:05.784 --> 00:02:09.522
This happens through a hardware mechanism
called a trap.

32
00:02:09.522 --> 00:02:15.083
Traps are not just for system calls; they also
handle device interrupts and memory faults.

33
00:02:15.083 --> 00:02:21.046
When a trap occurs, the CPU automatically
switches to kernel mode and jumps to a

34
00:02:21.046 --> 00:02:22.238
specific handler routine.

35
00:02:22.238 --> 00:02:28.961
Imagine a doorbell ringing in a house: the
resident (user mode) is interrupted, and the

36
00:02:28.961 --> 00:02:32.098
homeowner (kernel) comes to check the source.

37
00:02:32.098 --> 00:02:38.233
This unified trap mechanism simplifies OS
design because the same hardware signal can

38
00:02:38.233 --> 00:02:42.009
trigger different software responses depending
on the context.

39
00:02:42.009 --> 00:02:48.489
Whether it is a keyboard press, a disk error,
or a network request, the trap ensures the

40
00:02:48.489 --> 00:02:51.919
kernel intercepts the event before it disrupts
normal execution.

41
00:02:52.034 --> 00:02:59.142
Now, examine how the OS maps the vast virtual
address space of a program to limited physical

42
00:02:59.142 --> 00:02:59.560
memory.

43
00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:04.573
Each process maintains its own page table,
which acts like a detailed directory.

44
00:03:04.573 --> 00:03:10.607
When the program accesses a specific virtual
address, the hardware consults this table to

45
00:03:10.607 --> 00:03:12.762
find the corresponding physical location.

46
00:03:12.762 --> 00:03:18.533
If the page is not currently loaded in RAM,
the kernel triggers a page fault and loads the

47
00:03:18.533 --> 00:03:19.815
required data from disk.

48
00:03:19.815 --> 00:03:25.640
This translation happens transparently,
allowing programs to use large, contiguous

49
00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:30.300
address spaces without worrying about physical
memory constraints.

50
00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:36.103
Imagine a library catalog that translates
shelf locations into actual room numbers.

51
00:03:36.103 --> 00:03:42.226
Without this system, programs would need to
manually calculate every memory address,

52
00:03:42.226 --> 00:03:44.267
making development nearly impossible.

53
00:03:44.267 --> 00:03:50.314
The page table ensures that virtual addresses
remain stable while physical memory is

54
00:03:50.314 --> 00:03:51.244
dynamically managed.

55
00:03:51.386 --> 00:03:58.147
Virtual memory extends this translation system
to guarantee strict process isolation.

56
00:03:58.147 --> 00:04:04.632
Each process receives its own private address
space, meaning one program cannot accidentally

57
00:04:04.632 --> 00:04:07.126
or maliciously read another's data.

58
00:04:07.126 --> 00:04:13.069
The kernel controls which physical pages are
mapped to which virtual addresses, allowing it

59
00:04:13.069 --> 00:04:14.767
to enforce security boundaries.

60
00:04:14.767 --> 00:04:20.933
When a process terminates, the kernel simply
removes its page table entries, instantly

61
00:04:20.933 --> 00:04:22.356
freeing the memory.

62
00:04:22.356 --> 00:04:28.267
This design enables multiple applications to
run simultaneously without interfering with

63
00:04:28.267 --> 00:04:30.417
each other's data structures.

64
00:04:30.417 --> 00:04:35.840
Imagine separate rooms in a building where
each resident has their own private space, but

65
00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:38.733
the building manager controls which rooms are
open.

66
00:04:38.733 --> 00:04:44.372
This isolation prevents data corruption and
ensures system stability, even when

67
00:04:44.372 --> 00:04:46.935
applications crash or behave unpredictably.

68
00:04:47.066 --> 00:04:50.434
Let's trace how a new process begins
execution.

69
00:04:50.434 --> 00:04:56.392
When a program calls fork, the kernel creates
a new process image that is a logical copy of

70
00:04:56.392 --> 00:04:57.054
the parent.

71
00:04:57.054 --> 00:05:02.743
The child receives a unique process identifier
and starts executing independently.

72
00:05:02.743 --> 00:05:07.653
This duplication happens at the process level,
not the memory level.

73
00:05:07.653 --> 00:05:14.204
Imagine a photocopy machine that duplicates
the entire document but allows the copy to be

74
00:05:14.204 --> 00:05:15.077
edited separately.

75
00:05:15.077 --> 00:05:21.674
The parent and child now follow different
execution paths, each managing its own

76
00:05:21.674 --> 00:05:22.181
resources.

77
00:05:22.181 --> 00:05:27.092
This mechanism enables parallel execution and
modular program design.

78
00:05:27.092 --> 00:05:33.192
The kernel ensures that the child process has
all the necessary initialization data to start

79
00:05:33.192 --> 00:05:37.258
correctly, while maintaining strict separation
from the parent's subsequent operations.

80
00:05:37.370 --> 00:05:43.731
After the fork operation completes, the parent
and child processes receive different return

81
00:05:43.731 --> 00:05:44.220
values.

82
00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:50.223
This distinction allows each process to
immediately determine its role and choose its

83
00:05:50.223 --> 00:05:51.146
execution path.

84
00:05:51.146 --> 00:05:56.481
The parent typically continues with its
original task, while the child branches into a

85
00:05:56.481 --> 00:05:57.243
new routine.

86
00:05:57.243 --> 00:06:02.615
Imagine a traffic light system where the main
controller sends a signal to a side car,

87
00:06:02.615 --> 00:06:05.636
allowing the side car to take a different
route.

88
00:06:05.636 --> 00:06:11.743
This return value mechanism enables flexible
program architecture, allowing developers to

89
00:06:11.743 --> 00:06:16.184
implement complex workflows without hardcoding
every possible scenario.

90
00:06:16.184 --> 00:06:21.984
The kernel ensures that the return values are
correctly passed through the system call

91
00:06:21.984 --> 00:06:25.712
interface, maintaining the expected behavior
across different operating systems.

92
00:06:25.850 --> 00:06:31.923
When a process is created, it inherits certain
resources from its parent, such as file

93
00:06:31.923 --> 00:06:33.542
descriptors and memory mappings.

94
00:06:33.542 --> 00:06:39.129
These inherited resources follow strict
sharing rules that determine whether they are

95
00:06:39.129 --> 00:06:40.991
duplicated, shared, or restricted.

96
00:06:40.991 --> 00:06:47.156
Imagine a family recipe book where each family
member gets a copy, but some pages are marked

97
00:06:47.156 --> 00:06:48.606
as shared for collaboration.

98
00:06:48.606 --> 00:06:54.014
The kernel enforces these rules to prevent
resource leaks and ensure proper cleanup.

99
00:06:54.014 --> 00:06:59.172
If a parent closes a file descriptor, the
child may still access it depending on the

100
00:06:59.172 --> 00:06:59.817
sharing mode.

101
00:06:59.817 --> 00:07:05.966
This inheritance model allows applications to
pass resources between processes efficiently

102
00:07:05.966 --> 00:07:09.320
while maintaining control over access
permissions.

103
00:07:09.320 --> 00:07:13.822
The kernel tracks these references to ensure
they are released when no longer needed.

104
00:07:13.970 --> 00:07:19.366
To improve efficiency, many systems implement
copy-on-write virtual memory.

105
00:07:19.366 --> 00:07:25.480
Instead of duplicating all memory pages when a
process forks, the kernel shares the same

106
00:07:25.480 --> 00:07:27.925
physical pages between parent and child.

107
00:07:27.925 --> 00:07:31.700
Pages are only copied when one process
attempts to write to them.

108
00:07:31.700 --> 00:07:38.187
Imagine two readers sharing a single textbook;
they can read simultaneously without

109
00:07:38.187 --> 00:07:38.728
duplication.

110
00:07:38.728 --> 00:07:45.399
If one reader tries to edit a page, the system
creates a private copy for that specific page.

111
00:07:45.399 --> 00:07:50.743
This optimization reduces memory usage and
speeds up process creation.

112
00:07:50.743 --> 00:07:56.429
However, the kernel must carefully track write
attempts to ensure the original data remains

113
00:07:56.429 --> 00:07:58.460
intact for the other process.

114
00:07:58.460 --> 00:08:04.557
This approach preserves the expected process
behavior while maximizing resource efficiency.

115
00:08:04.682 --> 00:08:10.052
Finally, consider the complete lifecycle of a
process from creation to termination.

116
00:08:10.052 --> 00:08:15.425
The kernel manages execution replacement,
waiting states, and termination events,

117
00:08:15.425 --> 00:08:18.649
ensuring that resources are properly
reclaimed.

118
00:08:18.649 --> 00:08:24.840
If a process crashes or exits unexpectedly,
the kernel must handle the failure gracefully,

119
00:08:24.840 --> 00:08:27.935
cleaning up memory and releasing file
descriptors.

120
00:08:27.935 --> 00:08:34.016
Imagine a theater production where actors
leave early; the stage manager must reset the

121
00:08:34.016 --> 00:08:36.188
set and notify other performers.

122
00:08:36.188 --> 00:08:42.501
The kernel tracks every resource ownership and
failure case to maintain system stability.

123
00:08:42.501 --> 00:08:48.501
This disciplined lifecycle management prevents
memory leaks and ensures that the operating

124
00:08:48.501 --> 00:08:52.501
system remains responsive even when
applications encounter errors.
